 To assess whether diagnostically valid quiet windows are rare or fragile in applied registry data, we performed a systematic scan of quiet-window placements in the Czech Republic 2021–2024 cohort (enrollment ISO week 2021-24). We re-estimated the revised frailty parameters $(\hat{k}_d,\hat{\theta}_{0,d})$ across a sequence of overlapping quiet windows of fixed duration, holding all other settings fixed.
 
-Specifically, we considered 12-month windows beginning at successive monthly offsets between April 2022 and April 2023 (inclusive). For each placement we jointly fitted baseline rate and frailty using the same survivor law as the main method, with supplied sensitivity alpha=1.18. The quiet fit includes the four early post-skip anchors outside the marked wave. We retained the original enrollment time scale throughout. The pass rate counts fits accepted by the pipeline across all 13 attempted windows per group, including failures in the denominator. Results are shown for two birth-decade strata (1940s and 1950s), and for dose groups $d \in \{0,1,2\}$.
-
-Figure @fig:si_quiet_window_theta_scan shows fitted $\hat{\theta}_{0,d}$ as a function of quiet-window midpoint date. All six groups passed the pipeline diagnostics in all 13 windows (78 of 78 fits). The estimated frailty variance still depended on window placement. For dose 1 the fitted ranges were 0.00–1.56 in the 1940s and 0.00–0.00 in the 1950s. The windows overlap, so these counts describe sensitivity to placement within the same data.
